---
// Page 1 of the deck: the full-bleed forest plate with the headline ranged
// left over it.
//
// The headline is ONE short line per language, not the three hand-broken ones
// it used to be. The long version spelled out the whole proposition — woodland,
// Japan, most prestigious villa destinations — over three lines, which read as
// a paragraph laid over a photograph rather than as a title, and said in
// sentences what the picture was already saying. What is left names the thing
// being sold and stops.
//
// Because it is a single line there is nothing to hand-break, so the old
// per-language line splitting is gone with it. If a headline ever needs a hard
// break again, author it as separate dictionary keys and render one .line-mask
// per line — do NOT add a runtime splitter, since the four languages break at
// completely different points and a character-count split would put them in
// the wrong place in at least three of them.
import Figure from './Figure.astro';
import { useTranslations, type Locale } from '../i18n';

interface Props {
    locale: Locale;
}

const { locale } = Astro.props;
const t = useTranslations(locale);
---

{/* The header is WHITE for the whole of this section.

    It used to start dark over the sky and flip to white a third of the way
    down, which was correct by measurement — the top of the plate is sunlit
    haze where dark text reads 8.6:1 and white reads 1.0, and the lower two
    thirds are forest where those numbers invert. No single colour serves both
    on the raw image.

    It flipped mid-section anyway, while the visitor was still looking at the
    first screen, and starting dark then going light read as a glitch rather
    than as a response to the picture. One colour for the whole hero is the
    call; the faint top scrim and the layered text-shadow below are what pay
    for it. Both are annotated where they are set. */}
<section id="top" class="relative h-[100svh] min-h-[38rem] w-full overflow-hidden bg-forest-deep">
    <Figure
        src="/images/hero-forest.jpg"
        alt={t.hero.alt}
        width={2560}
        height={1700}
        loading="eager"
        fetchpriority="high"
        tone="forest"
        class="absolute inset-0 h-full w-full"
        imgClass="h-full w-full object-cover"
        data-hero-img
    />

    {/* Bottom-weighted scrim, anchoring the headline. */}
    <div
        class="pointer-events-none absolute inset-0"
        style="background: linear-gradient(to top, rgba(26,29,22,0.82) 0%, rgba(26,29,22,0.35) 42%, rgba(26,29,22,0.04) 78%, rgba(26,29,22,0.10) 100%);"
    >
    </div>

    {/* A very light top filter. This is a LOOK, not a legibility fix.

        At 0.30 it is barely perceptible as a band — it settles the brightest
        haze at the top of the frame and gives the bar something to sit
        against, which reads as depth rather than as a gradient laid over the
        picture. That is the whole intent. Set it much heavier and it stops
        being subtle and starts being a grey stripe across the photograph;
        that version was built and rejected on sight.

        ⚠️ Do not mistake this for a contrast solution. The top-left of the
        plate is sunlit haze, so the white bar is sitting on near-white.
        Measured over the band the bar actually occupies:

              top α        wordmark   links
              0.00           1.13       1.39
              0.30 (now)     1.91       2.32
              0.55           3.26       3.84
              0.78           5.77       6.52   ← clears WCAG AA

        So the header over the hero is deliberately not a measured-contrast
        design. It holds on the soft text-shadow in Navigation.astro and on
        the haze being bright but even and low-detail. If AA over the hero is
        ever required — an accessibility audit, a procurement rule — this is
        the knob, and 0.78 is the value that gets there. Anything in between
        buys very little and costs the photograph. */}
    <div
        class="pointer-events-none absolute inset-x-0 top-0 h-72 md:h-80"
        aria-hidden="true"
        style="background: linear-gradient(to bottom, rgba(20,23,17,0.30) 0%, rgba(20,23,17,0.25) 18%, rgba(20,23,17,0.18) 38%, rgba(20,23,17,0.11) 58%, rgba(20,23,17,0.04) 78%, rgba(20,23,17,0) 100%);"
    >
    </div>

    {/* Colour sentinel — invisible, no layout, no paint.

        Covers the ENTIRE hero, so the header is white from the first frame
        rather than starting dark and flipping at 34%. That split existed
        because the top of the plate was left clean and white could not hold
        there; the scrim above removes the reason for it. One colour for the
        whole section also means the bar no longer changes colour while the
        visitor is reading the headline. */}
    <div
        data-nav-dark="photo"
        aria-hidden="true"
        class="pointer-events-none absolute inset-0"
    >
    </div>

    {/* The headline and the scroll cue are ONE row, aligned on their bottom
        edges — not two stacked blocks.

        With the place line removed there was nothing left to sit under the
        headline, and the cue's own row left a 48px gap below the last line
        before the corner. Putting both in a single `items-end` row closes that
        gap and lands the last line of the headline on the same baseline as the
        foot of the scroll rule, so the two opposite corners of the plate are
        set by the same horizontal. The text block then sits properly into the
        bottom-left corner instead of floating above it.

        The cue is `hidden … sm:flex`, so on a phone this row has a single
        child and the headline simply keeps the full width. */}
    <div class="relative z-10 mx-auto flex h-full max-w-shell flex-col justify-end px-6 pb-24 md:px-10 md:pb-28 lg:px-14 lg:pb-32">
        <div class="flex items-end justify-between gap-10">
            <div>
                <p data-hero class="eyebrow mb-7 text-brass">{t.hero.eyebrow}</p>

                {/* One line, so it can be set larger again.

                    The ceiling came down to 2.6rem while this was three long
                    lines — at any more than that the longest of them wrapped
                    and the headline turned into a four-line block that covered
                    the photograph. A single short line has no such limit: 3.4rem
                    reads as a title rather than a caption and still occupies
                    well under half the width, so the plate is never crowded.

                    The floor stays 1.62rem, the largest size at which the
                    English line holds in one piece on a 390px phone. Below
                    that width it wraps, and `text-wrap: balance` in global.css
                    keeps the break even rather than orphaning "estate". */}
                <h1
                    data-hero-lines
                    class="display max-w-[34ch] text-bone text-[clamp(1.62rem,4.6vw,3.4rem)]"
                >
                    <span class="line-mask"><span>{t.hero.heading}</span></span>
                </h1>
            </div>

            {/* The place name used to sit below the headline as a second serif
                line, "Old Karuizawa 旧軽井沢". It is gone: those words are
                already in the eyebrow above, in the header wordmark, in the
                Overview heading and in the footer, and a fifth repeat directly
                under the headline read as filler rather than emphasis.

                pb-1 nudges the rule's foot onto the headline's baseline rather
                than its descender box, which is where the eye reads the line as
                ending. */}
            <a
                href="#overview"
                data-hero
                data-scroll-cue
                class="scroll-cue group hidden shrink-0 items-center gap-4 pb-1 text-[0.65rem] tracking-[0.3em] uppercase text-bone/55 transition-colors duration-500 hover:text-bone sm:flex"
            >
                {t.hero.scroll}
                <span class="scroll-rail" aria-hidden="true">
                    <span class="scroll-tick"></span>
                </span>
            </a>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<style>
    /* ==================================================================
       SCROLL CUE
       ==================================================================
       A hairline with a pulse of light falling down it, rather than a
       bouncing arrow. The arrow is the obvious choice and the wrong one
       here — it points, and this page is already asking the eye to move
       downward through a headline and a photograph. A pulse suggests the
       same direction without competing.

       THREE THINGS MAKE IT READ AS LIGHT RATHER THAN AS A BLOCK:

       1. The rail is masked at both ends, so it fades out instead of
          stopping at a hard edge. Previously it was a solid 1px bar with
          `overflow: hidden`, and the tick appeared and vanished at two
          visible cut lines — which is what made it look mechanical.
       2. The tick is a GRADIENT, transparent at both ends with brass in
          the middle, so it has no edges of its own to clip.
       3. It fades in as it enters and out as it leaves, on top of the
          movement. A pulse that is fully opaque for its whole travel
          reads as an object sliding; one that resolves at both ends
          reads as light passing.

       The long tail on the timing (the pulse lands at 62% and the cycle
       runs to 100%) is a deliberate rest. Without it this loops
       continuously and starts to feel like a loading indicator. */

    .scroll-rail {
        position: relative;
        display: block;
        width: 1px;
        height: 3.5rem;
        overflow: hidden;
        background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-bone) 22%, transparent);
        /* Fades the rail's own ends. Both properties are set because
           Safari still needs the prefix here. */
        -webkit-mask-image: linear-gradient(to bottom, transparent, #000 18%, #000 82%, transparent);
        mask-image: linear-gradient(to bottom, transparent, #000 18%, #000 82%, transparent);
        transition: background-color 0.5s var(--ease-estate);
    }

    .scroll-tick {
        position: absolute;
        inset-inline: 0;
        top: 0;
        display: block;
        height: 1.75rem;
        /* A PLATEAU, not a single peak. A gradient that hits full brass at
           one point only is invisible on a 1px element — there is nothing
           for the eye to catch. The solid core carries the pulse; the fades
           at either end are what stop it having edges. */
        background: linear-gradient(
            to bottom,
            transparent,
            var(--color-brass) 34%,
            var(--color-brass) 66%,
            transparent
        );
        /* On a 1px wire this bloom IS most of the effect. Without it the
           pulse is a hairline; with it, it reads as light with a little
           spill around it. */
        box-shadow: 0 0 7px 1px color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-brass) 45%, transparent);
        animation: scroll-tick 2.9s cubic-bezier(0.45, 0, 0.25, 1) infinite;
    }

    @keyframes scroll-tick {
        0% {
            transform: translateY(-110%);
            opacity: 0;
        }
        22% {
            opacity: 1;
        }
        52% {
            opacity: 1;
        }
        62%,
        100% {
            transform: translateY(300%);
            opacity: 0;
        }
    }

    /* Hover brightens the whole cue and lets the pulse travel a little
       further, so the control answers the pointer. The rail is not
       re-timed on hover — changing an animation's duration mid-cycle
       makes the pulse jump, which is worse than not responding at all. */
    .scroll-cue:hover .scroll-rail {
        background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-bone) 42%, transparent);
    }

    /* Reduced motion: park the pulse, visible, in the middle of the rail.
       The global override in global.css collapses every animation to its
       final frame, and this one's final frame is off the end of the rail
       at zero opacity — which would leave an empty hairline and no cue at
       all. Better to show a static mark. */
    @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
        .scroll-tick {
            animation: none;
            transform: translateY(50%);
            opacity: 1;
        }
    }
</style>
